# Geneva: The Beautiful City That Nurtures Virtue
Tamil title: ஜினிவா - அறம் வளர்க்கும் அழகூர்

A streak of light amid pitch darkness! A smile amid the frenzy of war! A loving glance amid the atrocities of domination! A mountain of virtue calling the makers of atom bombs to the path of righteousness! Geneva!

For the past two days, wherever in the world they lived, people watched with intense concern and anxious expectation the peace talks taking place at Geneva. They watched in Korea, where Syngman Rhee strutted and crowed like a hero of the battlefield; in Indo-China, where the victorious Ho Chi Minh fought valiantly; and in every capital, whether Moscow, Peking, Tokyo, Washington, Delhi or London.

Would Geneva show the way to prevent another dreadful world war? Would Geneva teach good sense to the great powers that crowed in reliance upon their stockpiled atom bombs? Would the powers abandon the monstrous work of cutting down humankind after war fever had gone to their heads? Would Geneva give them that path of virtue? Every person of understanding waited from minute to minute, opening the newspapers to read the news Geneva supplied.

There came a stage when one had to say sorrowfully, "There is no result. It is failure. What will become of the world's future?" People had reason to ask in disgust how arrogant men possessed by the desire for domination could ever renounce war, when smoke and blood were the very spectacles they delighted in, and how such men could possibly make peace talks succeed.

Yet Geneva has finally achieved a measure of success in restraining the imperialists. It is not a complete victory, but the talks have borne enough fruit for us to say, "It is not bad; the road to peace can be seen." Geneva appears as a sacred city offering hope to the world.

Not only today, but for a very long time, Geneva has shone as a beautiful city that nurtures virtue. Its history has unfolded in a manner capable of making virtue and love flourish in every heart.

Geneva is the third-largest city in Switzerland. Situated near the place where waters meet, it possesses a long history: stories of standing in the front rank for liberty, episodes singing victory songs for reason, and happy accounts of giving refuge to the persecuted.

From the age of Julius Caesar, who set out to rule the world beneath one umbrella and won many victories, to the age of Hitler, who dared to set out to Nazify the world, Geneva has remained a sacred city that smiled in the face of storms. Whenever white doves escaped the hunter's arrow and came to it, that garden served as their sanctuary.

Julius Caesar planted his standard of victory, raised temples of many kinds, and made Geneva an ornamental slave station of Rome. But Geneva fought vigorously for liberty, broke its chains, and won self-government.

Every time a mighty imperialist unleashed turmoil upon the European continent, Geneva stood like the lamp of a lighthouse guiding ships caught in a raging sea and unable to find their direction.

"God honours and blesses only truth, purity and service, you ignorant people! You will not receive God's grace by buying indulgences sold by a wrongdoer who dons a mendicant's garb and makes kingdoms dance to his will. Tear them up! Burn them! Do not buy indulgences and do not surrender your reason!"

It was at Geneva that the reformer Zwingli raised this cry.

When a pope who wallowed in luxury manufactured cartloads of indulgences and sold them in many countries through preachers, courageous men such as Zwingli condemned that injustice, endured cruelty, fought for knowledge, love and truth, and prevailed. It was at Geneva that they established a seat of learning and spread an ethical path.

When those persecuted because of the sixteenth-century revolution in Christianity came seeking refuge from France and Italy, it was this beautiful city that gave them a loving home.

The renowned Calvin established his camp at Geneva and defeated the authoritarianism of the Catholic Church. Before John Knox gained immense influence in Scotland and England, Geneva was also the place where he found refuge.

Calvin transformed the Geneva that sheltered him into a fortress of knowledge. Through his rare labour, the city became so celebrated that the position arose: Rome for Catholics, Geneva for Protestants!

Calvin also founded a university there to train teachers of religion. Its fame spread, and students came from throughout Europe to study. Even now Geneva serves as a training ground for international affairs.

In wars ignited by imperialists, ordinary people on both sides become soldiers and are sacrificed. When they lie half-dead and writhing on the battlefield, while great vultures circle to peck at them, is there no way to save them? Is there no means at least to lessen the suffering caused by the horror of war?

It was in Geneva that Dr Henry Dunant, moved by such thoughts, founded the Red Cross. Even while war is under way today, Red Cross workers can treat the wounded, and neither side opposes their work.

This pure service began in Geneva. Anguished by the consequences of a terrible war, the Swiss Henry Dunant gathered people of character who valued love and virtue, held a conference in 1864, and made possible the birth of the Red Cross. Geneva was the cradle of this worldwide organisation.

After the world war kindled by the German Kaiser, the first effort for world peace also began in Geneva. The League of Nations was established there. Though the League was not as powerful as today's United Nations, it worked with many good purposes.

Even now the International Labour Organization and the World Health Organization are in Geneva. It is also reported that Soviet Russia has agreed to join the International Labour Organization.

During wars, mail for prisoners of war accumulates in Geneva and is sent to the prisoners from there.

This beautiful city that nurtures virtue also possesses subtle technical skill. Watches, woollens, blankets, costly footwear, silks and garments with ornamental embroidery are flourishing crafts there.

If the leaders of the great powers who repeatedly gathered and spoke at Geneva had absorbed the precious lesson supplied by its history, we might see a world without war. Let us wait and see.

(*Dravida Nadu*, publication date damaged in the source)

---NOTES---
The OCR title begins with damaged numbering and “ஏினிவா”; the repeated name and subject establish ஜினிவா, Geneva. The text's “சிங்மன்ரீ” refers to Syngman Rhee and “ஹென்ரிட்யூனான்ட்” to Henry Dunant. The source loosely calls Dunant a doctor and later drops his surname; English uses his standard historical name but does not otherwise revise the argument. The final parenthetical date is illegible as “2 76.34,” so no conjectural date has been supplied.
